WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:04.060
- Thank you to Ancestry
for supporting PBS.

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:04.980
I don't know about you,

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:08.140
but I'm never thrilled
to go to the doctor.

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:10.280
Needles, sickness, injuries

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:12.820
and that crinkly paper
they make you sit on.

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:15.740
But the worst part is
the inevitable headache

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:17.910
of trying to pay for the visit.

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:19.790
- The American medical billing system

00:00:19.790 --> 00:00:23.070
is like putting salt in
our metaphorical wounds.

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:26.400
It's standard to have no idea
of what a procedure will cost

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:27.840
when we agree to it.

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:29.000
Then, six weeks later,

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:32.100
this piece of paper written
in an alien language

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:33.520
appears to inform you

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:36.060
that you owe such and
such for such and such,

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:38.150
and how would you like to pay for that?

00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:40.040
- If we want to engage productively

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:41.300
with our healthcare system,

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:43.810
we need to have a basic understanding

00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:45.900
of what's going on behind the scenes.

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:48.210
Only then can we protect our health

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:50.977
as well as our pocket books.

00:00:50.977 --> 00:00:53.560
(bright music)

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:00.460
- [Philip] When measured by GDP,

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:03.710
the US is one of the
wealthiest nations on earth.

00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:05.810
So it's understandable
that we, as a group,

00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:08.830
might pay more for the
healthcare we receive.

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:12.490
But when you compare what
we pay as a ratio to our GDP

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:15.860
to other similar countries,
we are an outlier.

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:18.120
We pay far more for our healthcare

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:20.230
than the size of our
economy would suggest.

00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:23.480
- [Julia] No shortage of
theories as to why that is.

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:26.020
Issues like defensive medical care,

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:27.770
bloated administrative costs,

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:31.390
higher doctor pay and drug
prices all contribute.

00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:33.700
But when you start
sorting through the mound

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:34.840
of complicated data,

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:38.760
it's really hard to single
out the clear villain.

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:40.570
Many healthcare experts believe

00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:43.470
that the main reason
our spending is so high

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:46.010
is because the price is high.

00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:48.400
Don't get that confused with the cost.

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:50.860
The cost of a good is the expense required

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:53.130
to make and provide the service.

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:54.730
The price is the amount

00:01:54.730 --> 00:01:57.210
that the customer is
willing to pay for it.

00:01:57.210 --> 00:01:59.830
In layman's terms, the
cost of our healthcare

00:01:59.830 --> 00:02:04.830
actually isn't unreasonably
high, but the price markup is.

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:06.970
- [Philip] Normally, a
seller's market occurs

00:02:06.970 --> 00:02:09.180
when demand exceeds supply.

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:11.560
This results in an
increase in competition,

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:13.050
which drives up the prices

00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:14.980
and allows the bulk of the pricing power

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:16.920
to be wielded by the seller.

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:20.320
Theoretically, a free market
economy could balance this

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:23.540
by allowing consumers to
not buy the product outright

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:25.490
or buy their products elsewhere.

00:02:25.490 --> 00:02:28.780
And this happens to a degree
in the form of medical tourism.

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:30.010
But for most of us,

00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:33.550
flying to Mexico or Thailand
for a medical procedure

00:02:33.550 --> 00:02:35.220
simply isn't an option.

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:39.140
And as for foregoing
medical care completely,

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:40.530
good luck with that.

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:42.500
- A critical factor in keeping it this way

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:45.440
is a criminal lack of pricing information.

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:46.300
Think about it.

00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:49.850
When do you finally know how
much your care costs you?

00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:53.280
Not til weeks after
you've already bought it.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:57.010
We also typically don't pay
for it directly ourselves,

00:02:57.010 --> 00:02:59.020
thanks to insurance companies.

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:00.290
Medical pricing sheets

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:04.870
are traditionally disclosed
only to them and not consumer.

00:03:04.870 --> 00:03:07.010
Imagine if you wanted to buy a house,

00:03:07.010 --> 00:03:10.570
but you couldn't see the
listing price before closing.

00:03:10.570 --> 00:03:12.920
Only your realtor, working on commission,

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:15.370
is allowed to see the price in advance.

00:03:15.370 --> 00:03:17.890
That's basically what's happening here.

00:03:17.890 --> 00:03:19.530
- The way other wealthy countries,

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:21.800
even those with large
private insurance companies,

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:25.650
have dealt with this is
through government regulation.

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:26.630
But for the most part,

00:03:26.630 --> 00:03:29.060
the US has completely stayed its hand.

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:32.407
Stanford economist, Victor
R. Fuchs said it this way:

00:03:32.407 --> 00:03:34.820
"Governments usually eliminate

00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:37.510
much of the high administrative
cost of insurance,

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:39.470
obtain lower prices for inputs

00:03:39.470 --> 00:03:41.220
while keeping tight control

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:43.350
on the number of specialist physicians

00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:44.970
and expensive technology.

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:46.210
In the United States,

00:03:46.210 --> 00:03:49.170
the political system creates choke points

00:03:49.170 --> 00:03:50.610
for diverse interest groups

00:03:50.610 --> 00:03:53.290
to block or modify the government's role."

00:03:53.290 --> 00:03:56.220
Suddenly, this whole mess
and political hullabaloo

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:58.630
makes a lot more sense.

00:03:58.630 --> 00:04:01.720
- [Julia] The one large
exception to this is Medicare,

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:04.210
which is a government program
that covers medical costs

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:08.110
of those over 65 and some
younger folks with disabilities.

00:04:08.110 --> 00:04:10.930
This accounts for just under 20%

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:13.310
of the entire US population.

00:04:13.310 --> 00:04:15.350
So it shouldn't come as a surprise

00:04:15.350 --> 00:04:18.810
that when you have that much
medical care to negotiate for,

00:04:18.810 --> 00:04:21.203
you can create some serious leverage.

00:04:22.420 --> 00:04:23.590
A recent study showed

00:04:23.590 --> 00:04:26.380
that the same service at the same facility

00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:29.030
would have cost a privately insured person

00:04:29.030 --> 00:04:32.840
two and a half times more
than one covered by Medicare.

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:36.050
- Now, other than voting and
calling our representatives,

00:04:36.050 --> 00:04:37.250
which you should do,

00:04:37.250 --> 00:04:39.840
we don't have a ton of individual sway

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:42.290
when it comes to changing
things at the top.

00:04:42.290 --> 00:04:44.720
- But do have more power than we think

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:48.180
when it comes to the bills
that show up in our mailboxes.

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:49.460
- This is Kira.

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:53.160
And she's what you might think
of as an extreme couponer

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:55.320
when it comes to medical costs.

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:57.780
Let's see what we can
learn by following her.

00:04:57.780 --> 00:04:59.450
- When she got hired at her new job,

00:04:59.450 --> 00:05:01.260
she sat down to compare the cost

00:05:01.260 --> 00:05:04.070
of three separate plans available to her.

00:05:04.070 --> 00:05:07.870
She was a bit confused as to
what each plan actually covers,

00:05:07.870 --> 00:05:11.460
so she called her HR department
for a little guidance.

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:13.640
This is a critical step.

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:16.930
Though medical benefits are
really important to people,

00:05:16.930 --> 00:05:19.180
most don't actually understand

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:21.680
what their policy does or doesn't cover.

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:25.660
How can you effectively use
something you don't understand?

00:05:25.660 --> 00:05:27.230
- [Philip] Now it's time to make a plan

00:05:27.230 --> 00:05:29.730
for urgent and emergency care.

00:05:29.730 --> 00:05:31.850
Urgent care facilities
are for medical needs

00:05:31.850 --> 00:05:34.640
that can't wait to see a
physician, like a dog bite,

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:37.080
but aren't immediately life-threatening.

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:40.180
The ER should be used for
life-threatening needs.

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:42.780
These visits can get expensive in a hurry,

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:47.240
with the average ER visit
clocking in around $2,000.

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:48.650
Kira checks out Google maps

00:05:48.650 --> 00:05:51.420
and calls two to three places
that are closest to her house

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:53.620
to see if they accept her insurance.

00:05:53.620 --> 00:05:55.320
Now they're entered into her phone

00:05:55.320 --> 00:05:59.010
so she can easily speed dial
them if anything should happen.

00:05:59.010 --> 00:06:01.970
- [Julia] Third, Kira never
accepts a medical bill

00:06:01.970 --> 00:06:03.050
at face value.

00:06:03.050 --> 00:06:04.800
Every time she goes to the doctor

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:06.670
for anything more than a checkup,

00:06:06.670 --> 00:06:09.240
she calls and asks for an itemized bill

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:10.570
just to see if the things

00:06:10.570 --> 00:06:13.620
she's being billed for actually occurred.

00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:16.380
Billing errors are frighteningly common.

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:18.730
Experts estimate medical billing errors

00:06:18.730 --> 00:06:22.840
to be present 75 to 80% of the time.

00:06:22.840 --> 00:06:26.330
- Fourth, she actually
shops around for her care.

00:06:26.330 --> 00:06:27.810
For non-urgent procedures,

00:06:27.810 --> 00:06:29.670
she spends some time on websites,

00:06:29.670 --> 00:06:33.590
like healthcarebluebook.com
or fairhealth.org.

00:06:33.590 --> 00:06:36.710
These sites are like the Kelley
Blue Book for medical care,

00:06:36.710 --> 00:06:38.240
giving her average prices

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:40.640
for things like tests and procedures.

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:42.300
Kira compares the prices

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:44.270
and even calls the hospital ahead of time

00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:47.110
to settle on it before
she walks in the doors.

00:06:47.110 --> 00:06:47.943
That's right.

00:06:47.943 --> 00:06:50.920
You can actually negotiate
directly with the hospital

00:06:50.920 --> 00:06:51.760
ahead of time.

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:54.010
- Sometimes she finds
herself looking at a bill

00:06:54.010 --> 00:06:56.690
for a procedure she couldn't shop for.

00:06:56.690 --> 00:06:59.360
But many larger
institutions, like hospitals,

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:02.550
actually have medical
billing advocates on staff

00:07:02.550 --> 00:07:06.450
to help patients, but you
usually have to ask for them.

00:07:06.450 --> 00:07:09.650
And if Kira is experiencing
a financial hardship

00:07:09.650 --> 00:07:12.140
or was without insurance for a season,

00:07:12.140 --> 00:07:15.400
simply mentioning this can
help knock down the price.

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:18.530
There were even independent
third party advocates

00:07:18.530 --> 00:07:20.500
that you can hire yourself,

00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:22.060
which may well be worth it

00:07:22.060 --> 00:07:24.480
if you're facing an especially large bit.

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:28.370
- If navigating all this
feels overwhelming and unfair,

00:07:28.370 --> 00:07:30.120
you're right, it is.

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:33.430
Our medical system can leave
us feeling powerless and lost.

00:07:33.430 --> 00:07:36.210
But just because we might feel powerless

00:07:36.210 --> 00:07:38.140
doesn't mean it's totally true.

00:07:38.140 --> 00:07:40.770
- If the system treats
us more like consumers

00:07:40.770 --> 00:07:44.950
and not patients, then let's
take every opportunity we can

00:07:44.950 --> 00:07:48.590
to be the savvy shoppers
we know we can be.

00:07:48.590 --> 00:07:50.270
- [Both] And that's our two cents.

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:53.320
- Thank you to Ancestry
for supporting PBS.

00:07:53.320 --> 00:07:56.610
Was that your great-grandmother
keeping the family together?

00:07:56.610 --> 00:08:00.290
Was that your great-grandfather
paving the way for change?

00:08:00.290 --> 00:08:03.950
Did they brave mother nature
and walk away stronger?

00:08:03.950 --> 00:08:07.920
Did they face the unknown
with resolve and triumph?

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:10.590
There is strength in every family story

00:08:10.590 --> 00:08:13.480
and Ancestry can help
you learn about yours.

